of this

12
00:00:48,179 --> 00:00:51,740
section is to explain different
perspectives on these words,

13
00:00:51,979 --> 00:00:56,299
what specific safety strategies aim to
achieve, and establish

14
00:00:56,299 --> 00:01:01,149
how our text will utilize them. AI Safety
Here

15
00:01:01,149 --> 00:01:07,069
is a definition of AI ensuring that AI
systems

16
00:01:07,150 --> 00:01:12,510
do not inadvertently or deliberately cause
harm or danger

17
00:01:12,510 --> 00:01:17,150
to humans or the environment through
research that identifies

18
00:01:17,150 --> 00:01:22,510
causes of unintended AI behavior and
develops tools for

19
00:01:22,590 --> 00:01:28,060
safe and reliable operation. AI safety
ensures AI systems

20
00:01:28,060 --> 00:01:30,700
do not cause harm to humans or the
environment.

21
00:01:31,340 --> 00:01:35,060
It encompasses the broadest range of
research and engineering

22
00:01:35,060 --> 00:01:39,500
practices focused on preventing harmful
outcomes from AI systems.

23
00:01:39,900 --> 00:01:43,739
While alignment focuses on aspects such as
an AI's

24
00:01:43,739 --> 00:01:47,780
goals and intentions, safety addresses a
broader range of

25
00:01:47,780 --> 00:01:51,420
concerns. It is concerned with ensuring
that AI systems

26
00:01:51,420 --> 00:01:55,659
do not inadvertently or deliberately cause
harm or danger

27
00:01:55,659 --> 00:01:59,920
to humans or the environment. AI safety
research seeks

28
00:01:59,920 --> 00:02:03,719
to identify the causes of unintended AI
behavior and

29
00:02:03,719 --> 00:02:07,840
develop tools for ensuring safe and
reliable operation. It

30
00:02:07,840 --> 00:02:12,599
can include technical subfields like
robustness ensuring reliable performance,

31
00:02:12,840 --> 00:02:18,439
including against adversarial attacks
Monitoring observing AI behavior and

32
00:02:18,520 --> 00:02:25,330
capability control limiting potentially
dangerous abilities AI Alignment the

33
00:02:25,330 --> 00:02:30,210
term AI alignment refers to the the
problem of

34
00:02:30,210 --> 00:02:33,689
building machines that faithfully try to
do what we

35
00:02:33,689 --> 00:02:36,530
want them to do or what we ought to

36
00:02:36,530 --> 00:02:40,289
want them to do. AI alignment aims to
ensure

37
00:02:40,530 --> 00:02:44,409
AI systems act in accordance with human
intentions and

38
00:02:44,409 --> 00:02:49,050
values. Alignment is a subset of safety
that focuses

39
00:02:49,050 --> 00:02:53,250
specifically on the technical problem of
ensuring AI objectives

40
00:02:53,490 --> 00:02:58,870
align human intentions and values.
Theoretically, a system could

41
00:02:58,870 --> 00:03:03,550
be aligned but unsafe, for example
Competently pursuing the

42
00:03:03,550 --> 00:03:08,990
wrong goal due to misspecification or safe
but unaligned,

43
00:03:09,069 --> 00:03:14,590
for example constrained by control
mechanisms despite misaligned objectives.

44
00:03:15,150 --> 00:03:20,430
While this sounds straightforward, the
precise scope varies significantly

45
00:03:20,430 --> 00:03:23,979
across research communities, but we
already saw a brief

46
00:03:23,979 --> 00:03:27,460
definition of alignment in the previous
chapter, but this

47
00:03:27,460 --> 00:03:30,860
section offers a more nuanced perspective
on the various

48
00:03:30,860 --> 00:03:34,979
definitions that we could potentially work
with. The textbook

49
00:03:34,979 --> 00:03:37,819
has a supplementary note titled what Do We
Mean

50
00:03:37,819 --> 00:03:41,979
by Alignment? Which we'll skip. Let's
define AI ethics,

51
00:03:42,460 --> 00:03:45,819
the study and application of moral
principles to AI

52
00:03:45,819 --> 00:03:50,930
development and deployment, addressing
questions of fairness, transparency,
accountability,

53
00:03:51,330 --> 00:03:55,129
privacy, autonomy, and other human values
that AI systems

54
00:03:55,129 --> 00:03:58,289
should respect or promote. AI ethics is
the field

55
00:03:58,289 --> 00:04:01,889
that examines the moral principles and
societal implications of

56
00:04:01,889 --> 00:04:06,530
AI systems. It addresses the ethical
considerations of potential

57
00:04:06,530 --> 00:04:10,689
societal upheavals resulting from AI
advancements and the moral

58
00:04:10,689 --> 00:04:14,370
frameworks necessary to navigate these
changes. The core of

59
00:04:14,370 --> 00:04:17,769
AI ethics lies in ensuring that AI
developments are

60
00:04:17,769 --> 00:04:21,670
aligned with human dignity, fairness, and
societal well being

61
00:04:21,829 --> 00:04:24,949
through a deep understanding of their
broader societal impact.

62
00:04:25,750 --> 00:04:29,870
Research in AI ethics would encompass, for
example, privacy

63
00:04:29,870 --> 00:04:34,949
norms. Identifying and mitigating bias in
systems ethics complements

64
00:04:34,949 --> 00:04:38,870
technical safety approaches by providing
normative guidance on what

65
00:04:38,870 --> 00:04:43,990
constitutes beneficial AI outcomes.
Alignment focuses on ensuring AI

66
00:04:43,990 --> 00:04:48,480
systems pursue intended objectives.
Research in ethics focuses on

67
00:04:48,480 --> 00:04:52,279
which objectives are worth pursuing. AI
ethics might also

68
00:04:52,279 --> 00:04:55,639
include discussions of digital rights and
potentially even the

69
00:04:55,639 --> 00:04:58,480
rights of digital minds and AIs in the
future.

70
00:04:58,959 --> 00:05:02,718
This chapter focuses primarily on safety
frameworks as they

71
00:05:02,718 --> 00:05:07,000
inform technical, safety, and governance
strategies rather than exploring

72
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:11,680
ethics, meta ethics, or digital rights.
Section. AI Control

73
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:15,970
we define AI control as follows. The
technical and

74
00:05:15,970 --> 00:05:20,170
procedural measures designed to prevent AI
systems from causing

75
00:05:20,170 --> 00:05:24,449
unacceptable outcomes, even if these
systems actively attempt to

76
00:05:24,449 --> 00:05:29,129
subvert safety measures. Control focuses
on maintaining human oversight

77
00:05:29,209 --> 00:05:32,610
regardless of whether the AI's objectives
align with human

78
00:05:32,610 --> 00:05:37,050
intentions. AI control ensures systems
remain under human authority

79
00:05:37,209 --> 00:05:42,029
despite potential misalignment. AI control
implements mechanisms such as

80
00:05:42,100 --> 00:05:45,339
to ensure AI systems remain under human
direction even

81
00:05:45,339 --> 00:05:49,259
when they might act against our interests.
Unlike alignment

82
00:05:49,259 --> 00:05:52,139
approaches that focus on giving AI systems
the right

83
00:05:52,139 --> 00:05:56,060
goals, control addresses what happens if
those goals diverge

84
00:05:56,060 --> 00:06:00,339
from human intentions. Control and
alignment work as complementary

85
00:06:00,339 --> 00:06:05,060
safety approaches, while alignment aims to
prevent preference divergence.

86
00:06:05,220 --> 00:06:08,959
By designing systems with the right
objectives, control creates

87
00:06:08,959 --> 00:06:12,279
layers of security that function even when
alignment fails.

88
00:06:12,920 --> 00:06:17,720
Control measures include monitoring AI
actions, restricting system capabilities,

89
00:06:18,120 --> 00:06:22,680
human auditing processes, and mechanisms
to terminate AI systems

90
00:06:22,680 --> 00:06:26,560
when necessary. Some researchers argue
that even if alignment

91
00:06:26,560 --> 00:06:31,000
is needed for superintelligence level AIs,
control through monitoring

92
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:33,959
may be a working strategy for less capable
systems.

93
00:06:34,970 --> 00:06:38,290
Ideally, an AGI would be aligned and
controllable, meaning

94
00:06:38,290 --> 00:06:40,250
it would have the right goals and be
subject

95
00:06:40,250 --> 00:06:43,370
to human oversight and intervention if
something goes wrong.

96
00:06:43,610 --> 00:06:46,610
The control line of AI safety work is
discussed

97
00:06:46,610 --> 00:06:49,290
in much more detail in our chapter on AI

98
00:06:49,290 --> 00:06:50,170
evaluations.
